{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-66202",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933758/assets/5f2e6c5d15161d02edc1ce014d926447cf4915e4b00b84b8c3935f9fbd8408d4.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin\u2061x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis.\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=\\frac{sin\u2061(-x)}{2}.$\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{sin\u2061(-x)}{2}=-\\frac{sin\u2061(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-\\frac{sin\u2061(x)}{2}.$ However, $y=-\\frac{sin\u2061(x)}{2}$ is not among the given options.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
